  "id": "COMPOUND:3995 - PROTEIN:1479",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that 4-hydroxybenzoic acid may be involved in the regulation of the Sodium channel subunit beta-2 through multiple indirect pathways. The first pathway links 4-hydroxybenzoic acid to Thiopurine S-methyltransferase, which is targeted by Tasumatrol I(rel), and ultimately to the Sodium channel subunit beta-2. The second and third pathways connect 4-hydroxybenzoic acid to DNA gyrase subunit B, which is targeted by Longispinogenin and Amidepsine D, both of which also target the Sodium channel subunit beta-2. These findings indicate that 4-hydroxybenzoic acid may act as a modulator of the Sodium channel subunit beta-2 through different molecular mechanisms, possibly by influencing enzyme activity or gene expression. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, suggesting a potential role for 4-hydroxybenzoic acid in the regulation of sodium channel function, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
